WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.501
-Our next guest
is a Grammy Award

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:03.701
winning musician and songwriter

00:00:03.701 --> 00:00:05.567
and frontman
of the band Wilco.

00:00:05.567 --> 00:00:07.434
His book,
"World Within a Song --

00:00:07.434 --> 00:00:09.701
Music That Changed My Life
and Life That Changed My Music"

00:00:09.701 --> 00:00:11.167
is available now.
Please welcome back to the show,

00:00:11.167 --> 00:00:13.434
our friend Jeff Tweedy,
everybody.

00:00:13.434 --> 00:00:16.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.434 --> 00:00:23.300
♪♪

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:30.167
♪♪

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:31.767
Welcome back.

00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:33.200
-Thanks for having me.
-It's lovely.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.367
Thank you for doing
the jingle to our podcast.

00:00:36.367 --> 00:00:37.834
-Oh. You're welcome.

00:00:37.834 --> 00:00:38.868
[ Laughter ]

00:00:38.868 --> 00:00:40.634
-I feel like you
maybe forgot you did it.

00:00:40.634 --> 00:00:41.667
-I did.
-Yeah?

00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:42.667
Alright, well,
thanks for your honesty.

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:44.033
-Is it good?
-It's great.

00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:45.534
-Yeah.
-Are you --

00:00:45.534 --> 00:00:48.100
You're obviously not getting
as much feedback on it as I am.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:52.067
-Oh. No. I like haven't. Yeah.
No, somebody told me.

00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:53.334
No. Nobody told me.
-Okay.

00:00:53.334 --> 00:00:54.634
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:56.467
I love this book because --

00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:59.133
And you've written others.
But this is a very unique take.

00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:02.734
This is the songs,
individual songs where you write

00:01:02.734 --> 00:01:04.601
just a few pages about
how important they are to you,

00:01:04.601 --> 00:01:06.734
but you did not open it

00:01:06.734 --> 00:01:09.534
with a very sort of hard
sell to the reader

00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:11.868
as far as how special
an experience this would be.

00:01:11.868 --> 00:01:13.601
Can I read you
the very first paragraph?

00:01:13.601 --> 00:01:15.634
-Sure.
-Jeff Tweedy out of the gate.

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:18.467
This is very absent
of braggadocio.

00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:20.567
"I'm going to level with you
right off the bat.

00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:22.100
I don't know what I'm doing,

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.267
and I probably don't have any
business writing another book,

00:01:25.267 --> 00:01:28.200
much less one is conceptually
conceived and philosophical,

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.501
as this one aims to be."

00:01:29.501 --> 00:01:31.000
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.200
So now, why do you want to have
people read that right away?

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.334
[ Laughter ]

00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:41.367
-It's -- It's honest for one.
-Yeah, it is.

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:45.501
-But you know,
I actually read a review.

00:01:45.501 --> 00:01:47.801
The only review
I've read of this book

00:01:47.801 --> 00:01:50.767
said that they wish
I wouldn't be so humble.

00:01:50.767 --> 00:01:51.767
-Oh.

00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:53.033
-And I sent it
to my family.

00:01:53.033 --> 00:01:54.601
And I said, "See,
I've always told you

00:01:54.601 --> 00:01:57.868
that my humbleness
was my biggest weakness."

00:01:57.868 --> 00:02:00.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:01.634
-One of the things

00:02:01.634 --> 00:02:03.467
you actually show
some humility about

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:06.601
is there are songs you loved
the first time you heard them,

00:02:06.601 --> 00:02:08.701
and I think we all have
that relationship with songs,

00:02:08.701 --> 00:02:11.133
but you also write
sort of a mea culpa

00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:13.367
about a few songs
that you didn't like originally,

00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:17.067
and then you came to appreciate
how great a song it was.

00:02:17.067 --> 00:02:19.834
"Dancing Queen" by -- by ABBA.
-Right.

00:02:19.834 --> 00:02:21.300
Undeniable.
-Undeniable.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.767
But you denied for a long time.
-But I did deny.

00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:24.334
But I denied it. Yeah.

00:02:24.334 --> 00:02:27.200
-So how did it dawn on you
that you were wrong?

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.334
-Um.

00:02:29.334 --> 00:02:32.901
I mean, a lot of things
started falling away

00:02:32.901 --> 00:02:36.200
once I got out of
the early adolescent

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.734
and adolescent period.

00:02:37.734 --> 00:02:42.033
And you start to realize that
there's a lot of great music,

00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:43.300
and it doesn't all have to fit

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.501
into your identity
and character.

00:02:46.501 --> 00:02:49.801
But, yeah, I was just standing
in the grocery store one day,

00:02:49.801 --> 00:02:51.868
and it was playing on
the speaker right above me,

00:02:51.868 --> 00:02:56.534
and I was just like, what is
this message from heaven?

00:02:56.534 --> 00:02:59.200
[ Laughter ]

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.701
I mean, it's so glorious.
It's so beautiful.

00:03:02.701 --> 00:03:07.234
Yeah. And I'm really happy
when things like that, like,

00:03:07.234 --> 00:03:10.434
reveal themselves
as their true nature.

00:03:10.434 --> 00:03:14.100
And you don't have
the whatever context

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.634
you originally heard it in
with people like threatening

00:03:16.634 --> 00:03:19.100
to beat you up
if you listen to it or,

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:22.067
you know, the different things
that we put in as barriers

00:03:22.067 --> 00:03:24.400
and they all fall away and you
can just listen to something,

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:25.834
it's really good for you.

00:03:25.834 --> 00:03:27.601
-I think it's also nice
when you point out in the book,

00:03:27.601 --> 00:03:29.667
if there's a song that all
of a sudden now you like,

00:03:29.667 --> 00:03:31.734
when you hear your world
just gets better,

00:03:31.734 --> 00:03:33.167
having songs you don't like

00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:35.567
is not a positive influence
on your life.

00:03:35.567 --> 00:03:38.234
It's way better to find love
in things.

00:03:38.234 --> 00:03:40.701
-Yeah. I mean, isn't that --
I mean, isn't that the goal?

00:03:40.701 --> 00:03:42.868
-That's the goal.
-Learn to love more, everybody.

00:03:42.868 --> 00:03:43.901
-Yeah.
-Come on.

00:03:43.901 --> 00:03:45.133
-"Dancing Queen"
is a real...

00:03:45.133 --> 00:03:46.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:46.501 --> 00:03:47.868
"Dancing Queen"...

00:03:47.868 --> 00:03:49.434
All right.

00:03:49.434 --> 00:03:50.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:50.734 --> 00:03:52.033
-I'm running for office.

00:03:52.033 --> 00:03:54.868
-Yeah, yeah, so take
that weird pro-love stance.

00:03:54.868 --> 00:03:56.267
-Yeah.
-Let's see how it goes.

00:03:56.267 --> 00:03:57.601
I think it's going to come back
to bite you.

00:03:57.601 --> 00:03:59.934
It's like --

00:03:59.934 --> 00:04:02.601
Yeah. Just floated it
out there like a test balloon.

00:04:02.601 --> 00:04:04.801
-Yeah. See how it polls.
-Yeah.

00:04:04.801 --> 00:04:07.100
-You talk about coming
to New York

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:08.601
when you were young
for the first time

00:04:08.601 --> 00:04:11.601
and seeing a street musicians

00:04:11.601 --> 00:04:13.968
and trying to bring back
their vibe,

00:04:13.968 --> 00:04:15.267
maybe a little bit
of their fashion

00:04:15.267 --> 00:04:17.100
when you went back
to to your Illinois,

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.901
your small Illinois town?

00:04:18.901 --> 00:04:20.834
-Well,
I was mostly impressed by --

00:04:20.834 --> 00:04:24.067
Well, first of all,
all of the musicians

00:04:24.067 --> 00:04:26.200
I would see on the street
were a million times better

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:27.400
than -- than I am now.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:28.801
-Right?

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:30.834
-I was really young and just
learning how to play the guitar,

00:04:30.834 --> 00:04:32.667
and I was like,
wow, I really suck

00:04:32.667 --> 00:04:34.067
and everybody's really great.

00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:36.000
What are they doing
in Carnegie Hall?

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.834
You know? But it wasn't
so much their fashion.

00:04:39.834 --> 00:04:43.300
It was just that they had
liberated themselves to be,

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:46.968
you know, artists
and play music for people

00:04:46.968 --> 00:04:49.968
and felt like confident,
and I wanted some of that.

00:04:49.968 --> 00:04:51.968
And so my mom
bought me a beret.

00:04:51.968 --> 00:04:53.734
-Wow.
-Yeah.

00:04:53.734 --> 00:04:55.868
-And now, were you
the first kid at your school

00:04:55.868 --> 00:04:58.834
in Illinois
to have a beret?

00:04:58.834 --> 00:05:02.701
-Not. Not that anybody
in my school knows.

00:05:02.701 --> 00:05:03.868
-Well, did you not
wear it to school?

00:05:03.868 --> 00:05:05.767
-No, no, I made it home
with the beret.

00:05:05.767 --> 00:05:09.100
-Okay.
-And I got out of my mom's car

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.767
with the beret on.
-To go to school.

00:05:10.767 --> 00:05:12.033
-To go to school.
-Yeah.

00:05:12.033 --> 00:05:15.067
Yeah. And quickly...

00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:16.934
[ Laughter ]

00:05:16.934 --> 00:05:18.601
-Did it just dawn on you
that it wasn't going to work?

00:05:18.601 --> 00:05:20.701
-I locked eyes with somebody

00:05:20.701 --> 00:05:24.200
and realized the colossal
mistake I was making.

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:25.534
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:25.534 --> 00:05:27.901
-That I was never going
to be able to pull this off.

00:05:27.901 --> 00:05:29.834
-And even now,
you don't beret it.

00:05:29.834 --> 00:05:31.601
-No, I mean, I'm wearing a --
Yeah.

00:05:31.601 --> 00:05:33.334
-Yeah.
-No, I think that there's Bel--

00:05:33.334 --> 00:05:35.400
-Nobody thinks
you're not pulling this off.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:37.200
-Oh, no.
-Yeah.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.300
-Anybody can pull this off.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:42.601
No, there's Belleville-Jeff,
and there's beret-Jeff.

00:05:42.601 --> 00:05:45.601
And -- And the Belleville's
where I grew up.

00:05:45.601 --> 00:05:48.100
And they're in a constant,
you know.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.701
[ Laughter ]

00:05:49.701 --> 00:05:52.868
Even to this day on stage
right now, at this very moment.

00:05:52.868 --> 00:05:53.901
-Yeah.
-One of them is like,

00:05:53.901 --> 00:05:55.634
"Who do you think you are?"
-Wow.

00:05:55.634 --> 00:05:58.000
-So you're telling me
that Beret-Jeff is inside you,

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.267
right now, wondering when
he's going to get his time.

00:06:00.267 --> 00:06:02.400
-Yeah, yeah.
Once you start, like,

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:03.868
talking about
how great the book is.

00:06:03.868 --> 00:06:05.000
[ Laughter ]

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:06.267
-Yeah. Here's the first
paragraph

00:06:06.267 --> 00:06:07.667
in beret-Jeff's
version is like,

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:09.601
"You're about to have
your mind [bleep] blown."

00:06:09.601 --> 00:06:12.634
-Exactly, exactly.
Yeah.

00:06:12.634 --> 00:06:15.634
This -- This book should
revolutionize literature.

00:06:15.634 --> 00:06:16.968
[ Laughter ]

00:06:16.968 --> 00:06:20.400
-Now you talk about
and Mavis Staples, for example,

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.334
is someone you got to talk to

00:06:22.334 --> 00:06:25.334
about how much one of her songs
meant to you.

00:06:25.334 --> 00:06:28.934
I would imagine you have people
who come up to you all the time

00:06:28.934 --> 00:06:34.267
to tell you a story about
one of your songs meant to them.

00:06:34.267 --> 00:06:36.834
-Yeah, like the theme song
for your podcast?

00:06:36.834 --> 00:06:38.100
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.534
And let me just say
I got everything I needed

00:06:39.534 --> 00:06:40.534
from you out of that.

00:06:40.534 --> 00:06:42.334
-Yeah.
-That was the dream.

00:06:42.334 --> 00:06:44.000
-That's an example
of the only one

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.133
that no one has come up to me
and talked to me about.

00:06:46.133 --> 00:06:47.801
-Alright.
-Yeah.

00:06:47.801 --> 00:06:49.200
[ Laughter ]

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.234
No, it's just...

00:06:50.234 --> 00:06:51.767
-Is it a weird
message to receive?

00:06:51.767 --> 00:06:53.534
-Oh, it's such a --
Yeah, it's a really --

00:06:53.534 --> 00:06:57.033
it's a -- it's such a gift to,
to make something and,

00:06:57.033 --> 00:07:01.167
and then come to understand
that it really did find somebody

00:07:01.167 --> 00:07:03.033
and mean something to somebody.

00:07:03.033 --> 00:07:05.701
And I'm going to cry
now talking about it. No.

00:07:05.701 --> 00:07:08.734
But it's really it's
a seriously beautiful thing

00:07:08.734 --> 00:07:10.133
to get to be a part of.

00:07:10.133 --> 00:07:14.234
But you also can't look at it
directly very long

00:07:14.234 --> 00:07:18.400
because it's not really
what got you to where you are.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.834
It's not. I didn't really think
that that would happen

00:07:21.834 --> 00:07:23.334
when I started writing songs,

00:07:23.334 --> 00:07:26.534
and I can't think about it
when I'm writing songs now,

00:07:26.534 --> 00:07:29.200
because that's just too much
weight to put on it, but.

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:30.801
-Yeah,
but it still must be nice.

00:07:30.801 --> 00:07:32.567
I mean, again, right? You can't
think about it at the time,

00:07:32.567 --> 00:07:35.334
but it must be just such
a wonderful sort of annuity

00:07:35.334 --> 00:07:36.734
to your work that someday

00:07:36.734 --> 00:07:38.634
somebody might come up to you
on any given song

00:07:38.634 --> 00:07:40.234
and just be like,
hey, this meant this to me.

00:07:40.234 --> 00:07:41.734
-Oh, it's the best.

00:07:41.734 --> 00:07:43.400
I mean, it's -- I always say,
that's what it's for.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:46.267
I mean, it is kind of like
the highest aspiration

00:07:46.267 --> 00:07:48.033
is that you could,
you know,

00:07:48.033 --> 00:07:51.667
keep somebody company in a,
in a, in a moment

00:07:51.667 --> 00:07:54.400
where they need some,
some consolation, you know.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.267
And then music does that
for all of us, I think.

00:07:57.267 --> 00:07:58.901
-Well, it's a wonderful book.

00:07:58.901 --> 00:08:02.501
The last Wilco album
came out September, also great.

00:08:02.501 --> 00:08:03.901
-Thank you so much.

00:08:03.901 --> 00:08:05.200
-And it is just
lovely to see you.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:06.334
I'm going to see you later
on tonight as well.

00:08:06.334 --> 00:08:07.901
-That's right.
-Double date.

00:08:07.901 --> 00:08:08.968
Jeff Tweedy, everybody.

00:08:08.968 --> 00:08:11.601
"World Within a Song"
is available now.

00:08:11.601 --> 00:08:12.968
We'll be right back
with Ms. Pat.

